            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the distance from the smallest value to the largest: the maximum minus the minimum. ptp stands for \"peak to peak\".\n\nIt is the simplest measure of how much something varies. Over a window of recent samples it says how far a sensor has moved, how loud a signal swings, how much a joint has travelled - and near zero means stillness. Because it rests entirely on the two most extreme values, a single glitch sets it; np.std or np.percentile give a steadier measure of spread when the data has outliers.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.ptp <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.ptp 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is measured and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone measures over every element. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one range per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nptp:\nThe range: a single value, or one per column or row.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 3 9 1 7 runs from 1 to 9, a range of 8.\n\nBottom: the same idea column by column. The 3 x 2 array has columns 1 4 2 (range 3) and 5 6 5 (range 1). A metro re-sends both five times a second; edit a message and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.amax and np.amin give the two ends separately. np.std measures spread from every value, not just the two extremes. np.percentile 5 and 95 give a range that ignores the most extreme tenth.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the distance from the smallest value to the largest: the maximum minus the minimum. ptp stands for \"peak to peak\".\n\nIt is the simplest measure of how much something varies. Over a window of recent samples it says how far a sensor has moved, how loud a signal swings, how much a joint has travelled - and near zero means stillness. Because it rests entirely on the two most extreme values, a single glitch sets it; np.std or np.percentile give a steadier measure of spread when the data has outliers.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.ptp <axis: int>\nDefault: axis None (the whole array).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.ptp 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is measured and sent.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nNone measures over every element. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one range per column, 1 one per row.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nptp:\nThe range: a single value, or one per column or row.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 3 9 1 7 runs from 1 to 9, a range of 8.\n\nBottom: the same idea column by column. The 3 x 2 array has columns 1 4 2 (range 3) and 5 6 5 (range 1). A metro re-sends both five times a second; edit a message and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.amax and np.amin give the two ends separately. np.std measures spread from every value, not just the two extremes. np.percentile 5 and 95 give a range that ignores the most extreme tenth."
        },
